# Set the port to listen on for incoming VPN packets.
# Remote nodes will automatically be informed about the new port number,
# but you might want to configure this in order to set external firewall
# settings.
# 2026-08-22: moved off Tailscale's well-known 41641.
# Evidence for carrier shaping on 41641: throughput to a cellular peer capped at
# ~1 Mbps whether 1 stream or 6 ran in parallel (6 delivered LESS aggregate than
# 1 - a shared pot, not loss-induced collapse), with loss 0-5%, jitter 10ms, and
# no MTU wall. Same cap over IPv4-CGNAT and native IPv6. Ookla on the same radio,
# same minute: 310 Mbps. Low loss + low jitter + hard ceiling = shaping.
#
# 4500/udp is IPsec NAT-T, which carriers rarely shape (it would break corporate
# VPNs). Revert: set this back to 41641 and restart tailscaled.
PORT="4500"

# Extra flags you might want to pass to tailscaled.
FLAGS=""

# REVERTED 2026-08-20. TS_DEBUG_MTU=1200 was set on 2026-08-18 for a cellular
# PMTU blackhole (inner MTU measured at 1256B; Stirling's 1MB JS bundle died at
# 343KB, Sonarr's bundles hit the same wall).
#
# It broke everything else off-wifi. IPv6 has a hard minimum link MTU of 1280,
# so at 1200 the kernel refuses to configure IPv6 on tailscale0 -- the interface
# had no v6 address at all, while Tailscale kept advertising the node's ULA and
# MagicDNS kept serving the AAAA. iOS prefers IPv6, so every connection tried a
# black hole first and waited out the timeout before falling back to v4. Jay and
# Maria saw Homepage, Lidarr, Plex and Seerr time out on cellular; wifi was fine
# because *.lan resolves to LAN IPv4 and never touches the tailnet.
#
# Do not lower this below 1280 again. The supported remedy for the blackhole is
# net.ipv4.tcp_mtu_probing=1 (already set, /etc/sysctl.d/99-tailscale-mtu.conf),
# which makes TCP shrink its own segments when it detects the wall. If the
# cellular stall returns, cap IPv4 tailnet traffic with a route-level MTU
# instead, which leaves the interface legal for IPv6.
# TS_DEBUG_MTU=1200
